  "narrative": "No assessments are due. All future estimates will be interpreted as already signed to the stated hypothesis direction. For dependency_composite, a scientifically meaningful dataset-specific association requires a signed point estimate above 0.15 composite dependency-score units and uncertainty supporting a threshold-exceeding effect in the claimed direction. The mature dataset-specific associations are: SMARCA4 loss toward stronger dependency; TP53 loss toward weaker dependency; KEAP1 loss toward weaker dependency; and MSI-high toward stronger dependency. STK11 is now closed after validation. PTEN remains unresolved and not accepted; further PTEN stratum mining is deferred because prior validation failed to clear threshold and additional contexts would increase multiplicity without clarifying uncertainty. I have therefore deferred many stale low-priority active claims, including PTEN branches and untested context interactions that are either lower-plausibility or redundant with already tested context axes. These deferrals are allocation decisions only and do not alter their scientific status.\n\nThis iteration registers a focused set of high-priority follow-up comparisons. First, because MSI-high is newly validated and strong, the registered robustness refinements H106-H109 should test whether the MSI-high association persists in low versus high mutation burden, TP53-intact samples, and high screen-quality samples. Second, I register interactions of the three large accepted associations with high mutation burden: SMARCA4-loss-by-high-mutation-burden, TP53-loss-by-high-mutation-burden, and KEAP1-loss-by-high-mutation-burden. For SMARCA4, direction remains -1 because the raw association is toward stronger dependency; a positive signed interaction above threshold would mean the stronger-dependency SMARCA4 association is amplified in high-TMB samples. For TP53 and KEAP1, direction is +1 because the raw associations are toward weaker dependency; a positive signed interaction would mean the weaker-dependency association is amplified in high-TMB samples. These tests are useful because high mutation burden is mechanistically and possibly confounding-related to MSI-high, so they assess whether the accepted effects differ by genomic instability state.\n\nI also register an MSI-high-by-stemness interaction and main effects for measured continuous predictors that could be biological or technical modifiers: high versus low mutation burden, high versus low aneuploidy score, high versus low Cas9 activity score, and high versus low growth rate. The mutation-burden main effect is important for interpreting MSI-high: if high mutation burden alone has a threshold-level stronger-dependency association, part of the MSI-high effect may reflect TMB rather than MSI status specifically. The aneuploidy and growth-rate comparisons test whether genome-wide genomic instability or proliferative capacity are associated with dependency scores; the expected directions are not strong, so these should be interpreted cautiously. The Cas9 activity comparison tests a technical confounding pathway: higher editing activity could yield more negative measured dependency through screen dynamic range rather than biology. All comparisons are exploratory dataset-specific associations, not causal or generalizable claims.\n\nNo validation is requested now. The new claims should be screened at discovery first. If any produces a large, interval-supported threshold effect in the claimed direction and is not simply a redundant main effect, validation could then be considered. The analysis priority for the next allocation is H106-H109 plus the eight newly registered claims, because these address MSI robustness, accepted-effect context dependence by high-TMB, and remaining high-value biological/technical predictors while avoiding PTEN over-mining.",
