  "narrative": "These are the final preplanned context tests for PTEN and CDKN2A. H215 is a clear positive result: within TP53/KEAP1/MSI-negative cells with lower screen quality, PTEN loss has signed estimate +0.336 [0.194, 0.479] (R217), excluding the 0.15-unit threshold in the claimed direction. I accept this as additional dataset-specific support that the PTEN-loss association is not driven solely by higher-quality screens. H219 is consistent but not threshold-supported: signed estimate +0.238 [0.122, 0.354] (R221). The point estimate exceeds the threshold and is in the expected direction, but the 95% interval includes effects below 0.15, so I will not accept this subgroup claim independently; it supports robustness of the already validated mature-context PTEN association without warranting a separate high-screen-quality claim. H216 tests PTEN-by-adenocarcinoma interaction and gives signed estimate -0.052 [-0.265, 0.162] (R218), near null and in the wrong signed direction; this argues against lineage-specific enhancement and favors retaining the broader H192 PTEN claim rather than the adenocarcinoma-subset claim. H220 tests PTEN-by-CDKN2A interaction and gives signed estimate +0.061 [-0.183, 0.304] (R222), a small and imprecise point estimate compatible with no meaningful interaction; I reject as not supported and note it mirrors the earlier null CDKN2A-by-PTEN interaction. H218 gives signed estimate +0.215 [0.104, 0.326] (R220), above the threshold at the point estimate but with a lower bound below 0.15; this is borderline and does not justify acceptance. H217 is inconclusive because at least one comparison cell has fewer than 20 observations (R219), so it cannot support or refute the interaction. Overall, no validation requests are possible because none remain. The central PTEN conclusion remains H192 plus H215 robustness: stronger dependency in TP53-WT/KEAP1-WT/MSI-negative cells, with no supported adenocarcinoma or CDKN2A interaction. CDKN2A remains unsupported as a scientifically meaningful predictor: point estimates are often positive but intervals or cell constraints prevent threshold-supported acceptance. All conclusions are dataset-specific associations, not causal, mechanistic, or generalizable claims.",
